{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795541778,"nonce":"TlsZtpvbkxgrZ+02QzqMXe26xrw=","title":"Trackpad momentum leaks from an inner scroll to its parent when the pointer leaves the inner scroll","message":"Reported by the owner on 2026-11-24: after a glide down in a code editor inside a scrolling page, moving the mouse off the editor made the rest of the momentum scroll the page.\n\nCause: dispatch_scroll_chain sends every scroll to the innermost scrollable under the pointer, by design (no chaining at edges). Trackpad momentum arrives as more scroll events after the lift, so each one went to whatever was under the pointer at that moment. The gesture latch in scroll_gesture.rs only kept gestures for scroll handlers with no physics of their own (swipes), never for physics containers.\n\nFix: the gesture records the container that took its last scroll before the fingers lifted; during the momentum that follows, both dispatch paths send scrolls to it while it is in the tree. The finger phase still routes by pointer, so a diagonal scroll over the editor's code still reaches the inner horizontal scroll and the editor in turn. Wheels report no phases and are unchanged.\n\nAlso corrected the book's Nested Scrolling section, which said outer scrolls take over at boundaries; the code has not chained at edges.","files":null}]}